esponse.json()) as Payload','const raw: unknown = await response.json()\nconst value = PayloadSchema.parse(raw)'
    if category in {"Type Testing","Testing TypeScript Applications"}:
        return 'expectTypeOf(createUser({ name: "Ada" })).toEqualTypeOf<User>()','// @ts-expect-error numbers are invalid names\ncreateUser({ name: 123 })','const result = value as any','expectTypeOf(value).toMatchTypeOf<Expected>()'
    if category=="ESLint and Code Quality":
        return 'await saveUser(user)','void backgroundTask().catch(reportError)','backgroundTask()','void backgroundTask().catch(reportError)'
    if category=="Performance":
        return 'npx tsc --noEmit --extendedDiagnostics','npx tsc --generateTrace .trace','type ExpandForever<T> = { [K in keyof T]: ExpandForever<T[K]> }','measure first, name intermediate types, and simplify the public surface'
    if category in {"Architecture","Production Engineering"}:
        return 'type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: "UserId" }','type CreateUserResult = Result<User, DuplicateEmailError>','export type SharedModel = DatabaseRow & ApiResponse','map database rows to domain types and domain types to public DTOs'
    return 'type Input = { id: string; value: string }\nfunction process(input: Input): string {\n  return input.value.trim()\n}','type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E }','function process(input: any): any { return input.value }','function process(input: Input): string { return input.value.trim() }'

def refs(category):
    mapping={
      "Modules":[("TypeScript modules","https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/modules/reference.html"),("Node packages","https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html")],
      "tsconfig.json":[("TSConfig","https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/")],
      "Strictness Options":[("TSConfig","https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/")],
      "Project References":[("Project references","https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/project-references.html")],
      "TypeScript with Node.js":[("Node TypeScript","https://nodejs.org/api/typescript.html")],
      "TypeScript with React":[("React TypeScript","https://react.dev/learn/typescript")],
      "TypeScript with Vue":[("Vue TypeScript","https://vuejs.org/guide/typescript/overview.html")],
      "Runtime Validation":[("Zod","https://zod.dev/")],
      "ESLint and Code Quality":[("typescript-eslint","https://typescript-eslint.io/getting-started/typed-linting/")],
    }
    items=mapping.get(category,[("TypeScript Handbook","https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/intro.html")])
    return "## Official references\n\n"+"\n".join(f"- [{a}]({b})" for a,b in items)+"\n"

def lesson(topic,category):
    basic,practical,bad,good=examples(topic,category)
    visual=f'## Visual model\n\n```mermaid\n{DIAGRAMS[topic]}\n```\n\n' if topic in DIAGRAMS else ''
    defined=definition(topic,category)
    return f'''---
title: {q(topic)}
description: {q("Learn "+topic+" with runtime behavior, compiler reasoning, practical examples, debugging, production guidance, and exercises.")}
---

# {topic}

## What the concept is

{defined}

## Why the concept exists

It makes the relationship between JavaScript behavior and the checker explicit. Used well, {topic} reduces duplicated assumptions, improves editor feedback, and makes changes safer at module and team boundaries.

## Beginner mental model

Think of **{topic}** as a contract between code that produces a value and code that consumes it. The contract should prevent invalid operations without becoming harder to understand than the runtime behavior it models.

## JavaScript runtime behavior

JavaScript executes the emitted program. Prototype lookup, coercion, mutation, scheduling, module loading, I/O, and exceptions still follow JavaScript and platform rules. TypeScript does not add runtime validation unless the implementation performs it.

## TypeScript compile-time behavior

The checker combines {topic} with contextual types, control flow, declarations, and compiler options. It reports diagnostics and powers editor tooling; it does not execute the program.

{visual}## Basic TypeScript example

```ts
{basic}
```

Hover important expressions and compare inferred types with declared contracts.

## Practical application

```ts
{practical}
```

Keep contracts close to the boundary that owns them. Prefer inference locally and named types for important reusable boundaries.

## Incorrect design

```ts
{bad}
```

This loses information, hides uncertainty, or promises something runtime code has not proved.

## Safer design

```ts
{good}
```

The safer form preserves evidence. Isolate unavoidable assertions behind validation or a function that documents the invariant.

## Inference and compiler diagnostics

Identify the actual type, expected type, and first incompatible member. Decide whether the implementation, model, or boundary assumption is wrong before changing the final assignment. Preserve relevant compiler options in minimal reproductions.

## Common mistakes

- Treating a static contract as runtime validation.
- Adding `any`, a broad assertion, or `!` before identifying the broken assumption.
- Duplicating a contract instead of deriving it from the source of truth.
- Exposing an implementation type as a public API.
- Building a clever type that is harder to maintain than the bug it prevents.

## Debugging guidance

Start with `npx tsc --noEmit --pretty false`. Use `--showConfig` for configuration, `--traceResolution` for imports, and `--extendedDiagnostics` for performance. Confirm editor and CI versions match. Reduce the problem to the smallest failing declaration and assignment.

## Performance implications

Most uses of {topic} have no direct runtime cost because types are erased. Runtime schemas, imports, object creation, and emitted helpers do have costs. Huge unions, recursive conditional types, repeated mapped transformations, and large declaration surfaces can slow checking.

## API and library design implications

Public contracts should be stable, readable, and easy to infer. Avoid leaking private implementation details, database records, framework internals, or enormous computed types. Test accepted and rejected consumer usage.

## Production considerations

Run type checking as a separate CI gate even when a bundler strips TypeScript syntax. Validate external data at runtime, pin a compiler range, review release notes, and verify module behavior against deployment.

## Interview explanation

Explain **{topic}** in three parts: runtime behavior, compile-time behavior, and one trade-off. Use a minimal example, then connect it to an API or production boundary.

## Summary

- {defined}
- Static types improve feedback but do not replace runtime checks.
- Preserve inference and make invalid states harder to represent.
- Fix the underlying relationship before using an escape hatch.

## Practice exercises

1. Make the basic example reject an invalid value.
